WEBVTT

NOTE SE101 Lesson 10: Strict about data, relaxed about the perimeter — captions generated from the narration script

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.377
Software engineering 101. Lesson ten, the last one.

2
00:00:04.377 --> 00:00:07.950
Strict about data. Relaxed about the perimeter.

3
00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:13.002
Quick question. What's the safest way to protect a customer's card number?

4
00:00:13.002 --> 00:00:15.608
Encrypt it? Keep it on a private network?

5
00:00:15.608 --> 00:00:18.086
Or never hold it at all? Never hold it.

6
00:00:18.086 --> 00:00:20.310
You can't leak what you don't have.

7
00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:23.828
On security, I'm a pragmatist about most things.

8
00:00:23.828 --> 00:00:28.662
But I don't bend on two. One: customer personal data doesn't get exposed.

9
00:00:28.662 --> 00:00:32.370
Two: payment card data stays with the payment processor.

10
00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:35.502
Notice the second one isn't a better lock.

11
00:00:35.502 --> 00:00:39.013
It's a design decision. The processor holds the card.

12
00:00:39.013 --> 00:00:42.590
You hold a token. There's nothing here worth stealing.

13
00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:50.070
So I'll say this plainly. Unless payments is your business, keep your card handling as small as you possibly can.

14
00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:55.832
Now the pragmatic side. I'm comfortable putting an internal app on the public internet.

15
00:00:55.832 --> 00:00:58.009
As long as the front door is solid.

16
00:00:58.009 --> 00:01:00.000
Company sign-in, on every route.

17
00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:04.705
Why? Because nearly everything you use is already a service on the internet.

18
00:01:04.705 --> 00:01:08.020
The office network stopped being the wall a long time ago.

19
00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:11.450
The wall now is who you are, and what you're allowed to see.

20
00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:15.088
So. Strict about the data. Strict about identity.

21
00:01:15.088 --> 00:01:17.570
And relaxed about where the app sits.

22
00:01:17.570 --> 00:01:23.810
One more thing. Any change that touches personal data or payments gets an independent review.

23
00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:26.806
Rule ten, the last one, for your agents.

24
00:01:26.806 --> 00:01:29.452
Never store, log, or send raw card data.

25
00:01:29.452 --> 00:01:35.272
Customer personal data stays out of logs, errors, test data, and your own working notes.

26
00:01:35.272 --> 00:01:39.042
Internal apps sit behind company sign-in, on every route.

27
00:01:39.042 --> 00:01:44.400
And any change that touches personal data or payments gets an independent review.

28
00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:48.152
So here's the whole series, on one screen.

29
00:01:48.152 --> 00:01:52.619
One. Plan, build, verify, with an independent verifier.

30
00:01:52.619 --> 00:01:55.138
Two. Rigor follows risk. Three.

31
00:01:55.138 --> 00:01:57.737
Principles over languages. Four.

32
00:01:57.737 --> 00:02:02.205
Stay at the top of the ladder, and send the agent down.

33
00:02:02.205 --> 00:02:06.510
Five. Understand the problem before anything changes.

34
00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:09.141
Six. Say what, not how. Seven.

35
00:02:09.141 --> 00:02:13.306
The old practices still work, with agents in the seats.

36
00:02:13.306 --> 00:02:16.107
Eight. Same ceremonies, faster clock.

37
00:02:16.107 --> 00:02:18.681
Nine. Know what it costs. And ten.

38
00:02:18.681 --> 00:02:22.240
Strict about data, relaxed about the perimeter.

39
00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:25.211
And there's one idea underneath all ten.

40
00:02:25.211 --> 00:02:28.697
Every practice is a trade, and AI changed the prices.

41
00:02:28.697 --> 00:02:30.670
So some of my answers changed.

42
00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:34.879
More tests. Root cause, for anything that touches money or data.

43
00:02:34.879 --> 00:02:37.049
The whole sprint cycle, in a day.

44
00:02:37.049 --> 00:02:41.193
And some didn't. I still have to understand the problem myself.

45
00:02:41.193 --> 00:02:44.350
And the customer's data is still not negotiable.

46
00:02:44.710 --> 00:02:47.757
Most of this series was about one piece of work.

47
00:02:47.757 --> 00:02:50.676
One fix. One feature. Getting one thing right.

48
00:02:50.676 --> 00:02:53.723
Software Engineering 201 is about whole systems.

49
00:02:53.723 --> 00:02:56.706
How you design one. How the parts fit together.

50
00:02:56.706 --> 00:03:02.292
And how that has changed, from thirty years ago, to now, with agents doing the building.

51
00:03:02.292 --> 00:03:04.640
I'll use real systems I've worked on.

52
00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:06.958
So, one last question for you.

53
00:03:06.958 --> 00:03:09.859
Which of the ten rules changed how you work?

54
00:03:09.859 --> 00:03:12.430
And which one do you think I got wrong?

55
00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:15.260
See you in 201.
